**Drawing one number with probabilities $P_i$.** Let $r$ be uniform in $[0,1)$ and $S_i=P_1+\dots+P_i$ ($S_0=0$, $S_N=1$). Return the smallest $i$ with $r<S_i$. Then $i$ is returned exactly when $S_{i-1}\le r<S_i$, which has probability $S_i-S_{i-1}=P_i$.

**Choosing $K$ distinct numbers.** As the statement prescribes, we repeat the draw. A number that is already chosen is discarded and we draw again; a new number is recorded. We stop when $K$ numbers are recorded. (This terminates with probability 1 provided at least $K$ of the $P_i$ are positive, which is necessary for the task to make sense.)

```pascal
procedure Choose(K, N: integer; P: tR; var X: tN);
var
  used: array [1..N_MAX] of boolean;
  i, m: integer;
  r, s: real;
begin
  for i := 1 to N do used[i] := false;
  m := 0;                          { numbers chosen so far }
  while m < K do
  begin
    r := random;                   { uniform in [0,1) }
    s := 0; i := 0;
    repeat                         { smallest i with r < P[1]+...+P[i] }
      i := i + 1;
      s := s + P[i]
    until (r < s) or (i = N);      { i = N guards against rounding }
    if not used[i] then
    begin
      used[i] := true;
      m := m + 1;
      X[m] := i
    end
  end
end;
```

The guard `i = N` only matters if the floating-point sum of all $P_i$ comes out slightly below $1$. Each accepted number $i$ was drawn with probability $P_i$ among all draws, and draws of already-chosen numbers are simply repeated, exactly as required. The result is in `X[1..K]`.

Rejecting repeats is the same as drawing from the not-yet-chosen numbers with probabilities proportional to $P_i$. If $C$ is the set of chosen numbers, each unchosen $i$ is picked with probability $P_i/(1-\sum_{j\in C}P_j)$. So we can scale $r$ by the remaining mass and skip used indices:

```pascal
procedure Choose2(K, N: integer; P: tR; var X: tN);
var
  used: array [1..N_MAX] of boolean;
  i, m, last: integer;
  r, s, rest: real;
begin
  for i := 1 to N do used[i] := false;
  rest := 1;                       { total probability of unchosen numbers }
  for m := 1 to K do
  begin
    r := random * rest;
    s := 0; i := 0; last := 0;
    repeat
      i := i + 1;
      if not used[i] then
      begin
        s := s + P[i];
        if P[i] > 0 then last := i
      end
    until (r < s) or (i = N);
    if r >= s then i := last;      { rounding guard }
    used[i] := true;
    rest := rest - P[i];
    X[m] := i
  end
end;
```

Each step does at most $N$ iterations, so the running time is $O(KN)$ regardless of the probabilities.
